# What the Vercel function needs, and nothing else: the input of the lock.
#
# Vercel installs requirements.txt from the repository root (vercel.json's
# installCommand), because its Python builder does not read pyproject.toml
# from a subdirectory. That file is not written by hand. It is this list
# resolved to one exact version of every package, transitive ones included,
# for CPython 3.12 on Linux: .python-version's Python and CI's, on the
# platform the function runs on. While it held these floors instead, every
# deploy installed whatever was newest that day: SQLAlchemy 2.1 reached
# production and CI unannounced, and a checkout set up the week before went
# on testing 2.0 (#164, #192).
#
# After editing this file, regenerate the lock from the repository root with
# the command written in its header (`pip install uv` if you have no uv). uv
# rather than pip-compile, because it resolves for a Python and a platform
# other than the one it runs on, and pip-compile answers for the interpreter
# that runs it — on the owner's Windows machine, neither Linux nor 3.12.
# Without `--upgrade` it keeps every pin the edit does not force to move;
# `--upgrade-package NAME` moves one package, `--upgrade` moves them all.
# Keep the two names as they are: dependabot pairs the lock with its input by
# swapping .in for .txt, not by reading the header, whose order it cannot
# parse.
#
# CI installs the lock and the package in one resolve, so the suite runs on
# the versions the deployment gets, and a lock that no longer satisfies a
# floor fails CI's install rather than the deployment.
#
# The requirements are server/pyproject.toml's, character for character, and
# server/tests/test_requirements_mirror.py holds both that and the lock.
#
# asyncpg only: the serverless database is Postgres, and aiosqlite would be
# dead weight in the function bundle.
fastapi>=0.141.1
aiogram>=3.31.0
sqlalchemy[asyncio]>=2.1.1
asyncpg>=0.31.0
pydantic>=2.13.5
pydantic-settings>=2.15.0
# The DI container in server/app/di.py. Pure Python, and 26 ms on top of
# fastapi and sqlalchemy on a cold start — measured, because this bundle pays
# for every import on every one of them.
